          "settings": {
            "key": "buy",
            "step": "STEP 01",
            "title": "Before you buy",
            "chip_label": "Before you buy",
            "note": "What the patches are, what they realistically do, and who should skip them."
          }
        },
        "c1q1": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What exactly is a microdart patch?",
            "answer": "<p>It's a small crescent-shaped patch that sits under your eye. The underside is covered in hundreds of tiny cones made from dried, concentrated actives — not metal, not needles in the medical sense. They're shorter than a grain of salt.</p><p>When you press the patch on, the cones sit just inside the top layer of skin and dissolve over the next few hours, releasing what's in them where a cream can't easily go.</p>"
          }
        },
        "c1q2": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How is this different from the eye creams I've already tried?",
            "answer": "<p>A cream has to get through the outer barrier of your skin, which is the layer specifically designed to keep things out. Most of it doesn't make it far.</p><p>The dissolving cones start below that barrier. Same idea as the difference between rubbing something on a sealed surface and putting it just under the seal.</p><p>That's a delivery difference, not magic. If a cream failed you because the ingredient was wrong for your problem, a patch with the same wrong ingredient will also fail.</p>"
          }
        },
        "c1q3": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Will this work on bags I've had my whole life?",
            "answer": "<p>Honest answer: partly, and it depends what's actually under there.</p><p>If your under-eye looks heavy because of fluid, thin skin, or lost firmness, that's the kind of thing a topical can visibly improve. If it's inherited fat pads that push forward — the kind you can see in photos of you at seventeen — no patch, cream, or serum moves those. Only a surgeon does.</p><p>Most people are some mix of both. We'd rather tell you that now than take your money and let you find out in week six.</p>"
          }
        },
        "c1q4": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is it an alternative to surgery or filler?",
            "answer": "<p>No, and we won't claim it is. Blepharoplasty removes or repositions tissue. Filler adds volume underneath. Cebeam does neither.</p><p>What it can do is improve the look of the skin sitting on top — smoothness, firmness, tone. For plenty of people that's the difference between looking tired and looking rested. For some it isn't enough, and surgery is the honest answer.</p>"
          }
        },
        "c1q5": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Does it hurt?",
            "answer": "<p>You'll feel a light prickle or tingle for the first minute or two as the cones soften. After that you stop noticing the patch is there.</p><p>Sharp pain isn't normal. If you feel that, take the patch off.</p>"
          }
        },
        "c1q6": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "I'm a man and I don't do skincare. Is this going to be a whole routine?",
            "answer": "<p>No. Press two patches on, leave them, remove them. There's no cream, no order of steps, no bathroom shelf involved.</p><p>Plenty of our customers wear them while they're asleep and never think about it again.</p>"
          }
        },
        "c1q7": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Who shouldn't use these?",
            "answer": "<p>Skip them if you have broken, irritated, or sunburned skin around the eye, an active eye infection, or eczema or dermatitis in the area.</p><p>If you're pregnant or nursing, on prescription retinoids, or recovering from any procedure around the eyes, ask your doctor first. Not for anyone under 18.</p>"
          }
        },
        "c2": {
          "type": "category",
          "settings": {
            "key": "start",
            "step": "STEP 02",
            "title": "Your first 14 days",
            "chip_label": "First 14 days",
            "note": "How to apply them properly, what's normal early on, and when to expect anything visible."
          }
        },
        "c2q1": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How do I apply them?",
            "answer": "<p>Start with clean, completely dry skin — no serum, oil, or moisturiser underneath. Damp skin is the single most common reason a patch won't stick.</p><p>Place one under each eye, then press firmly with your fingertip for about 10 seconds. You want real pressure, not a pat. Leave on for at least 2 hours; overnight is better.</p><p>Remove slowly, and gently press whatever's left on the skin in. Don't wash it off.</p>"
          }
        },
        "c2q2": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "My patch keeps falling off.",
            "answer": "<p>Almost always moisture. Give your under-eye a full two minutes to air-dry after cleansing before you apply.</p><p>The other cause is not pressing hard enough. A light touch leaves the cones sitting on the surface instead of anchored in.</p>"
          }
        },
        "c2q3": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How often should I use them?",
            "answer": "<p>Every other night for the first two weeks, then two to three nights a week to maintain.</p><p>Using them every single night won't speed anything up — your skin needs the off-nights.</p>"
          }
        },
        "c2q4": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Some redness after I take them off — is that a problem?",
            "answer": "<p>Mild pink that fades within an hour is expected, especially the first few times. So is a slight grid pattern where the cones were.</p><p>What isn't expected: swelling, itching, burning, or redness still there the next morning. Stop using them and email us.</p>"
          }
        },
        "c2q5": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "When will I actually see something?",
            "answer": "<p>A short-term smoothing effect is often visible the morning after — that's mostly hydration, and it comes and goes.</p><p>Changes that hold generally take four to eight weeks of consistent use, because that's how long skin takes to turn over. If you're buying for a specific date, start at least six weeks out.</p><p>Results vary from person to person, and some people won't see a change they consider worth it. That's what the guarantee is for.</p>"
          }
        },
        "c3": {
          "type": "category",
          "settings": {
            "key": "use",
            "step": "STEP 03",
            "title": "Living with it",
            "chip_label": "Ongoing use",
            "note": "Makeup, other products, storage, and the questions that come up around month two."
          }
        },
        "c3q1": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I wear makeup over the area?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes — after you remove the patch, not with it on. Wait about 20 minutes so the skin isn't tacky, then apply concealer as normal.</p><p>A lot of people tell us concealer sits better on the days after, because it's sitting on smoother skin. That's the realistic version of the \"my concealer creases\" problem: you're not hiding the texture, you're changing it.</p>"
          }
        },
        "c3q2": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I use these with retinol or acids?",
            "answer": "<p>Not on the same night, and not on the same area. Both do their work in the same layer of skin, and stacking them is how people end up irritated.</p><p>Alternate nights works fine. If you're on prescription tretinoin, talk to whoever prescribed it first.</p>"
          }
        },
        "c3q3": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I use them on smile lines or my forehead?",
            "answer": "<p>The shape is cut for the under-eye curve, so it'll fit awkwardly elsewhere, but the formula itself is fine on other facial skin.</p><p>Don't put them on the eyelid itself, or anywhere the skin is broken.</p>"
          }
        },
        "c3q4": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How do I store them?",
            "answer": "<p>Sealed, somewhere cool and dry, out of direct sun. No fridge needed — and don't refrigerate, because condensation ruins the cones.</p><p>Once a patch is out of its pouch, use it. It starts absorbing moisture from the air right away.</p>"
          }
        },
        "c3q5": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What's actually in them?",
